{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1784648154,"nonce":"WmmOPmLXZeLUVcTTlev0UINpPuc=","message":"## Root-cause analysis\n\nTracking a new posting dedups only by matching the discovered siblings'\n(fingerprint, message-id) against already-tracked rows. When that match\nmisses — which it can for a version tracked earlier under its own\nchange-id — the code takes the \"not tracked\" branch and re-records the whole\ndiscovered set (v1/v2/v3) under a brand-new change-id, duplicating the v2\nthat was already tracked. Unlike the manual-link path, the track path has no\nfingerprint-based absorption to catch this.\n\n**The dedup logic** — `cmd track`, `src/b4/review/tracking.py:582-668`:\n\n1. First, an exact check for the *requested* posting (v3):\n   `SELECT change_id FROM series WHERE change_id = ? OR fingerprint = ?`\n   (lines 582-585). v3 isn't tracked → miss.\n2. Fallback: build `discovered` = `(fingerprint, first-patch-msgid)` for each\n   version in `lmbx.series` (lines 587-599), then\n   `find_existing_change_id(conn, discovered)` (line 600), which probes the\n   `series` and `revisions` tables by fingerprint and by message-id\n   (`tracking.py:1097-1120`).\n3. If it returns a change-id → fold the new revisions into that series\n   (line 606, \"already tracked\"). If it returns `None` → `add_series_to_db()`\n   with a fresh change-id and `_record_discovered_revisions()` records\n   v1/v2/v3 under it (lines 640-664) — the duplicate.\n\n**Why step 2 can miss the already-tracked v2.** The match hinges on the\ndiscovered v2 presenting the *same* fingerprint or first-patch msgid as the\nrow stored when v2 was tracked directly. The per-revision fingerprint folds\nin sender + revision + patch-ids, and the comment at `tracking.py:566-576`\n(bug 70fe607) already acknowledges that a re-derived revision's synthesized\nchange-id and fingerprint \"both differ from the tracked version.\" v1/v2 here\n\"had been single patches\" and were tracked on their own; reconstructed as\nsiblings of v3 they can fingerprint differently, and the msgid probe uses\nonly the *first* patch's msgid — so both keys can fail to line up with the\nstored v2. (This is the probable trigger; the exact divergence for this\nthread isn't proven from code alone.)\n\n**The missing safety net.** Even when a discovered sibling *is* already a\nseparately-tracked series, the track path never reconciles them:\n`absorb_series_as_revision()` (`tracking.py:1184`) is invoked **only** from\n`record_linked_revision()` — the manual-link path (`tracking.py:1350`).\n`_record_discovered_revisions()` on the track path checks only whether a\nrevision already exists *under the change-id being written*\n(`get_revisions(conn, change_id)`, `tracking.py:~1145`); it never asks\nwhether that revision already lives under a *different* change-id. So the\nmanual-link fingerprint absorption that would merge the stray v2 has no\nequivalent here, and the duplicate is created and left in place.\n\n## Related bugs\n\nThe false-negative discovery family: `47d5a4c` (new version not noticed),\n`78c0fa1` (fuzzy fallback *false-positive* match). The 0.16 manual-linking\nwork (fingerprint column + `absorb_series_as_revision`) is the machinery\nthat exists for exactly this reconciliation — it just isn't wired into the\nautomatic track path yet. No fix suggestions per request.","files":null}]}